{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "title": "findings@13",
  "description": "The RECONCILE document, replacing findings@11. findings@11 added a conditional requiring `open_severity` on any element lacking a settling `prior_disposition` ruling, enforced at the aggregate step. Engine rule V30 (internal/workflow/schemas.go) validates a declared aggregate-step schema by probing it with a synthetic element built only from that schema's own declared fields plus the four keys `aggregate` always writes (`severity`, `members`, `held`, `operator_resolved`); the probe carries neither `open_severity` nor `prior_disposition`, so it always falls into findings@11's \"open, no ruling\" branch and always fails — no `if/else` or `dependentRequired` on an aggregate-step schema can ever pass this probe, so findings@11 could never register. The check was also redundant by construction: findings-cluster@5, synthesize-findings' own payload, already requires `open_severity` on every open cluster before reconcile ever runs, and `aggregate` carries every non-severity key of its input through to its output verbatim (including `open_severity` and `prior_disposition`), so nothing reconcile's schema could check was ever at risk of going missing between the two steps. findings@13 is byte-identical to findings@10 in every constraint — same `required: [\"severity\"]`, same fields, same ladders, `additionalProperties` stays true — dropping only the unenforceable conditional. `open_severity` keeps `ordered_enum: true`: reconcile's own threshold predicates (`any(open_severity >= blocker)`, `any(open_severity >= high)`) are ordered comparisons, and the engine defines those only over a field whose registered schema declares that order (internal/engine/threshold.go). findings@11 stays registered for any run that already declared it; it is orphaned once every consuming workflow repoints here.",
  "type": "array",
  "items": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "id": {
        "description": "Stable identifier for the finding or cluster, unique within the payload. Not `required`: the builtin `aggregate` carries through only the input element's own keys, so a schema requiring `id` would make aggregate's pass-through payloads unregisterable.",
        "type": "string",
        "minLength": 1
      },
      "title": {
        "description": "One-line statement of the defect.",
        "type": "string",
        "minLength": 1
      },
      "severity": {
        "description": "The finding's severity, on 02 §6's five-value ladder. The enum's document order IS the order thresholds compare on (`any(severity >= high)`) and the order `aggregate` reduces over: info < low < medium < high < blocker. Judges author on the general or security ladder fragment and map onto these values at emit time.",
        "type": "string",
        "enum": ["info", "low", "medium", "high", "blocker"],
        "ordered_enum": true
      },
      "open_severity": {
        "description": "The cluster's severity WHILE IT REMAINS OPEN, as a scalar on the same five-value ladder as `severity` (max of the member severities for a multi-member cluster). Present ONLY on a cluster with no settling prior_disposition ruling; omitted on settled ground, so `any(open_severity >= high)` reads the open set and nothing else. Emitted by synthesize-findings (findings-cluster@5, which requires it on every open cluster) and carried through `aggregate` verbatim like every non-severity key. Not independently enforced here: findings-cluster@5 is upstream of every reconcile input, so a synthesize payload missing it on an open cluster is refused before it ever reaches this step.",
        "type": "string",
        "enum": ["info", "low", "medium", "high", "blocker"],
        "ordered_enum": true
      },
      "file": {
        "description": "Repo-relative path the finding is anchored to.",
        "type": "string"
      },
      "line": {
        "description": "1-indexed line within `file`. Optional per 02 §6. An explicit `null` is ALSO valid — synthesize-findings emits `line: null` for a finding whose scope is the whole file or the whole commit, where there is no single line to anchor to; that is distinct from simply omitting the key. `minimum` applies only when the value is a number, so the integer case stays constrained and null passes untouched.",
        "type": ["integer", "null"],
        "minimum": 1
      },
      "evidence": {
        "description": "What a skeptic can re-run or re-read to confirm the finding.",
        "type": "string"
      },
      "alternative": {
        "description": "A concrete alternative to the flagged approach. OPTIONAL at every severity: the judge-design rule pairing a blocker with an alternative is a prose obligation in that contract, and synthesize-findings — which emits this same payload and adds no alternative — would be refused by a schema-level conditional.",
        "type": "string"
      },
      "members": {
        "description": "The cluster's member severities, in arrival order. Present on the `aggregate` output ONLY. It is NOT how synthesize-findings carries a cluster: synthesize declares `payload = \"findings-cluster@5\"`, whose scalar-or-array `severity` is the `aggregate` builtin's INPUT contract, reading member severities from each input element's own `severity` field rather than from a `members` array; the linkage between elements of one cluster is carried in `member_ids` and `member_sources` (findings-cluster@5), not here. Values are the same ladder as `severity`; the constraint is not repeated as an `enum` here because `aggregate@1` leaves `members` unconstrained and the conjunction of the two documents must stay satisfiable.",
        "type": "array",
        "minItems": 1
      },
      "held": {
        "description": "Set by the builtin `aggregate` (06 §2): the cluster's spread reached `hold_spread`, so it is excluded from automatic routing and materializes a `<step>-held` human step. Absent on any step whose `aggregate` declares no `hold_spread`, since a hold can never trigger there.",
        "type": "boolean"
      },
      "demoted_from": {
        "description": "Set by `aggregate` when the computed value sits below the cluster's maximum member — the recorded demotion trail (02 §6). Absent when nothing was demoted, which today means absent everywhere: every aggregate step in the corpus declares `method = \"max\"`, and max never computes below its own maximum member. It stays declared so the trail keeps one meaning and stays pinned to the five-value ladder wherever one does appear — carried forward on a cluster an earlier, reducing method produced, or emitted again if a workflow ever reduces by anything other than max. `additionalProperties` is true, so dropping the declaration would leave the key unchecked rather than refused.",
        "type": "string",
        "enum": ["info", "low", "medium", "high", "blocker"]
      },
      "operator_resolved": {
        "description": "Set when an operator accepted a held cluster's value via `docket step approve|reject`; thresholds re-apply over the resolved set (02 §6). It records that THIS round's hold was decided, and does not survive into a later round — `prior_disposition` is what carries a decision forward.",
        "type": "boolean"
      },
      "prior_disposition": {
        "description": "How this same locus was already ruled on in an EARLIER fix-loop round. Set by synthesize-findings when its inputs identify a cluster as a re-occurrence, and carried through unchanged by `aggregate`. Its presence is the signal that the cluster is settled ground rather than a discovery: a reader — human or panel — owes it a re-read of the earlier ruling before spending a second decision. Absent on a genuinely new cluster, which is the ordinary case. Every sub-field is optional so a partially-known disposition is still recordable; an empty object records nothing and is no better than omitting the field. A re-review round's payload spans the STANDING SET, not the delta: a finding still open is carried forward with this key set even when it did not re-occur in that round's inputs, emitted as one element carrying the scalar the previous aggregate reduced it to rather than its original member array — spread 0, so `hold_spread` cannot re-hold ground an operator already ruled on.",
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "round": {
            "description": "The fix-loop round the ruling was made in, 0-based — round 0 is the first review, before any fix step ran.",
            "type": "integer",
            "minimum": 0
          },
          "ruling": {
            "description": "What was decided, in the decider's terms. Conventional forms: `accepted` (the computed value stood), `corrected-to-<severity>` (approved with `--value`), `rejected`, or `deferred` (accepted on the promise of later work, which `follow_up_issue` must then name).",
            "type": "string",
            "minLength": 1
          },
          "ruled_by": {
            "description": "Who decided: `operator`, or `panel <proposal-id>` where a tribunal did. A ruling with no name is not a ruling anyone can re-read.",
            "type": "string",
            "minLength": 1
          },
          "follow_up_issue": {
            "description": "The issue id the deferred work was filed as. REQUIRED IN PRACTICE when `ruling` is a deferral: a deferral with no issue is the evaporation this field exists to make visible. Not schema-required, because a schema-level conditional here would refuse payloads the aggregate legitimately carries through.",
            "type": "string",
            "minLength": 1
          }
        },
        "additionalProperties": true
      }
    },
    "required": ["severity"],
    "additionalProperties": true
  }
}

